{"decisions":[{"i":0,"decision":"F2","origin":"narrative","reason":"The figures are supported, but in the narrative this sentence follows the nitrogen-oxides component, so “Its phase I” materially implies that the phase-I unit counts belong to nitrogen oxides rather than sulfur dioxide.","sourceEvidence":"The source attributes the 1995 phase I, 263 initial units at 110 plants, and 445-unit total to the sulfur dioxide mechanism.","synthesisStatement":"“The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading… Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units.”","narrativeStatement":"After stating that the nitrogen oxides component began in 1996, the narrative says: “Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units.”","materiality":"Misattributes the phase-I scope and counts to the other pollutant, undermining the stated distinction between the two rules."},{"i":1,"decision":"F2","origin":"narrative","reason":"The dates and cap are supported for sulfur dioxide, but the preceding narrative sentence’s ambiguous “Its phase I” makes the endpoint read as continuing the nitrogen-oxides timeline.","sourceEvidence":"The source states that sulfur dioxide Phase II began in 2000 and that its final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons; nitrogen oxides did not introduce a cap.","synthesisStatement":"“Phase II began in 2000. The final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons.” This follows the sulfur dioxide phase-I account; the synthesis separately states that nitrogen oxides had no cap.","narrativeStatement":"“Phase II began in 2000. The final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons.” This follows the narrative’s claim that the preceding phase I was “Its” for the nitrogen oxides component.","materiality":"In context, it can imply that nitrogen oxides had a Phase II and a 2010 cap, contradicting the source’s distinction."}]}

